keyTakeaways:
  - Medinet Habu rises over the ground the Egyptians held to be the first hill
    out of the waters of chaos, the place where the world began.
  - Every ten days, for more than a thousand years, the god Amun was carried
    here to begin the world again.
  - The same ground held the graves of the first gods, so every visit was also
    a return to one's origins.
  - Its heart is not the great temple but the small temple of Amun inside the
    gate, the one most visitors walk straight past.
  - Pharaohs, priestesses, townspeople and, later, Christians kept this ground
    holy for more than two thousand years.
faqs:
  - question: Is Medinet Habu worth visiting?
    answer: >-
      Yes. It is one of the best-preserved temples in Thebes, with deep-cut
      reliefs and original colour still on its ceilings, and it is usually far
      quieter than Karnak or the Valley of the Kings. It is also one of the most
      meaningful: the Egyptians believed it stood on the ground where the world
      began.
  - question: Who built Medinet Habu?
    answer: >-
      The great temple was built by Ramesses III around 1175 BC as his
      "Mansion of Millions of Years". It wraps around a much older temple of
      Amun, rebuilt by Hatshepsut and Thutmose III on a shrine that stood here by
      the Middle Kingdom, around 2000 BC.
  - question: Why is Medinet Habu considered sacred?
    answer: >-
      Because of the ground it stands on. The people of Thebes believed this was
      the first mound that rose out of the primeval waters at the beginning of
      time, and the resting place of the eight first gods who existed before
      creation. A small temple of Amun was built over that spot, and Ramesses
      III later built his great temple around it.
  - question: What was the festival held every ten days?
    answer: >-
      The Egyptian week was ten days long. At the end of each one, the image of
      Amun was carried from Luxor Temple across the Nile to the small temple at
      Medinet Habu, to make offerings to the first gods and renew creation.
      Egyptologists call it the Decade Festival. It is attested from the New
      Kingdom and best documented in Late and Graeco-Roman times.
  - question: Who were the God's Wives of Amun?
    answer: >-
      Women, often the daughters of kings, who held the highest religious office
      in Thebes from around the 8th to the 6th century BC. They devoted their
      lives to Amun, did not marry, and passed the role on by adopting a
      successor. Their chapels stand just inside the gate at Medinet Habu.
  - question: Why does Medinet Habu look like a fortress?
    answer: >-
      Ramesses III built its entrance as a gatehouse modelled on a fortified
      Syrian tower, a migdol, probably more ceremonial than military. A mudbrick
      wall around the temple is thought to have stood about 18 metres high. The
      walls marked a clear
      boundary between the everyday world and the sacred ground inside, with the
      small temple of Amun, on the mound of creation, at its centre.
  - question: What do the battle scenes at Medinet Habu show?
    answer: >-
      The outer walls and the great gateway show Ramesses III's wars, including
      the king striking down his enemies before Amun and his victory over the Sea
      Peoples, one of the earliest sea battles ever depicted. Inside, the theme
      changes: captives are presented to the gods, and the inner courts turn to
      festivals and offerings. Egyptian temples placed battle scenes on the
      outside as a kind of shield, the king holding chaos at bay so order could
      live within.
  - question: Is there evidence for the energy and lost-technology claims made online?
    answer: >-
      Not in the ancient sources. What the Egyptians left us is a belief about
      creation, renewal and order, carved and written on these walls. Many people
      do feel a deep stillness here, and that experience is real; we simply
      don't attach claims to it that the Egyptians never made.
  - question: When is the best time to visit, and what does it cost?
    answer: >-
      Come at opening, at 6am, when it is cool and almost empty. The Ministry of
      Tourism and Antiquities lists the site as open daily from 6am to 5pm, with
      a ticket of about EGP 230 for foreign adults (EGP 110 for students). Hours
      and prices change, so we check them before every guest's day.
  - question: How do I get to Medinet Habu?
    answer: >-
      It is on Luxor's West Bank, about 15–30 minutes by car from central Luxor
      depending on traffic and where you cross the Nile. There is no simple
      public route to the gate, so most visitors come by taxi or private car; we
      arrange the car and the timing so you arrive at opening.
  - question: Can I take photos inside Medinet Habu?
    answer: >-
      Personal photos with a phone or camera are generally allowed, and
      Medinet Habu has not charged a separate camera fee. Tripods, drones and
      professional shoots need permits. Rules can change, so we confirm them
      before every guest's day.
  - question: How long should I spend there?
    answer: >-
      About two hours. Give the small temple and the chapels by the gate a slow
      half hour before you go into the great temple.
