metaTitle: "Medinet Habu: Where the World Began (Meaning & History)"
metaDescription: >-
  Why the Egyptians held Medinet Habu sacred: the first ground of creation,
  renewed every ten days, and the timeless ideas of balance and return behind it.
---
To the ancient Egyptians, Medinet Habu was the place where the world began.

Not a symbol of it. The place itself. The people of Thebes believed this ground on Luxor's West Bank was [the first hill to rise out of the dark waters](https://www.globalegyptianmuseum.org/glossary.aspx?id=235) at the dawn of time.

Kings, priestesses, farmers and pilgrims returned to this ground for more than two thousand years. The ideas that drew them here are as true now as they were then.

## Seven ideas to look for

Seven simple ideas are built into this temple. Keep them in mind as you walk:

- **Stillness.** Everything begins in quiet.
- **The hidden.** What matters most is kept small, and close.
- **Renewal.** Nothing stays new on its own.
- **Return.** What ends goes back to its source.
- **Balance.** Order has to be kept, every day.
- **Depth.** Carve deep what you want to last.
- **Strength.** Hold the line at the edges, and rule the kingdom within.

---

## Why Medinet Habu is more than a warrior's temple

Most people arrive, look up at the towering gateway and the carved battles, and see a warrior's temple.

They are right. And they are missing the point.

Ramesses III built all of this around something older and far smaller: a modest temple of Amun, just inside the gate on your right. It is the religious heart of the site, and it is where [our Medinet Habu mornings](/medinet-habu) always begin. Most visitors pass it in a minute.

The Egyptians would have started there.

## Everything begins in stillness

Before anything existed, the Egyptians said, there was only water. Dark, endless and still. They called it **Nun**.

Nun was not evil. It was everything that had not yet happened. Pure possibility, waiting for a shape.

Then a mound of earth rose out of the water. Light touched it. Life began. The Egyptians called that moment **the First Time**.

Walk into any of their temples and [you climb that first hill](https://www.memphis.edu/hypostyle/meaning_function/model-universe.php). The floor rises beneath you. The ceilings come lower. The light fades. With every step you move closer to the dark, quiet room where life began.

At Medinet Habu, the people of Thebes believed they stood on the hill itself.

You don't need to be Egyptian to recognise this. A seed opens in dark soil. A child grows in silence. A true decision is made in a quiet moment, long before anyone else hears it.

> Everything real begins in stillness.

## The hidden one

The god of this place was **Amun**. His name means "the hidden one".

That is why the holiest rooms in an Egyptian temple are the smallest and the darkest. What matters most is not put on display. It is kept quiet, and approached slowly.

The small temple at Medinet Habu keeps that truth. Plain on the outside. Inside, the oldest ground the Egyptians knew.

![A long carved passage under a painted ceiling at Medinet Habu](/images/articles/medinet-habu-meaning-passage.jpg)
*The deeper you walk, the quieter it becomes.*

## Begin again, every ten days

The world was not made once. It has to be made again.

The Egyptian week was ten days long. At the end of every week, priests lifted the image of Amun from [Luxor Temple](/experiences/luxor-temple), carried him across the Nile, and brought him up to the small temple on the sacred hill. There he made his offerings. And the world began again.

Every ten days. For [more than a thousand years](https://writeups.talesfromthetwolands.org/2022/07/26/the-temple-of-amun-at-medinet-habu-birth-place-and-burial-place-of-the-primordial-deities-lucia-gahlin/).

The Egyptians saw this rhythm everywhere. The sun sank each evening and was reborn each morning. The Nile flooded every summer, and as the water drew back, the black earth appeared again. Many scholars believe that very sight gave the Egyptians their image of the first hill rising out of Nun.

It is a kind way to look at a life. When something grows tired, it has not failed. It is asking to be renewed. A home, a friendship, a body, a faith: each needs to come back to its beginning now and then, and be cared for again.

> When something grows tired, it has not failed. It is asking to be renewed.

![Pillars carved as Ramesses III in the form of Osiris, at Medinet Habu](/images/articles/medinet-habu-meaning-osiris-pillars.jpg)
*The king carved as Osiris, [the god who died and rose again](/insiders-guide/isis-and-osiris-love-story).*

## Return to your roots

The sacred hill was a birthplace. It was also a grave.

The first gods, the eight who lived in the dark water before creation, were believed to rest inside it. So every ten days, when Amun came here, he was visiting his ancestors, at the place he was born.

Birth and death in the same ground. What ends goes back to its source. And from the source, life begins again.

Ordinary families lived this every day. They brought bread and water to their parents' tombs and spoke their names out loud. A name spoken, and an offering given, kept the dead cared for. The Egyptians feared a "second death", a final ending beyond this life, and remembering was part of how they guarded against it.

## Keep your balance

Around 1175 BC, Ramesses III built his temple here, wrapped around the sacred hill. He called it his **Mansion of Millions of Years**: the place where he would be honoured forever, beside the place where time began.

The outer walls show his battles. Read them only as war, and you miss what an Egyptian saw. A king's true duty was to keep **maat**: balance, truth, the right order of things. Chaos always waited at the edges, like the dark water before creation. Order never kept itself. Someone had to hold it, every single day.

Maat was not only for kings. It meant telling the truth. Being fair. Feeding the hungry. Keeping your word.

At the end of a life, the Egyptians believed, your heart was weighed against a single feather. One question remained.

> Did you live in balance?

![A painted column at Medinet Habu, its colours still bright](/images/articles/medinet-habu-meaning-painted-column.jpg)
*Where the sun never reached, the colour has lasted three thousand years.*

## Carve it deep

Ramesses III did not want a temple of surfaces. Much of Medinet Habu is cut in **deep sunk relief**: figures and hieroglyphs carved down into the sandstone, not raised from it. In places the signs are so deep that birds rest inside them. It is often said he wanted his name to be impossible to erase. Deep-cut signs also read beautifully in strong sun.

Stand there when the sun is high and you see why it works. Every sign fills with its own shadow. Three thousand years later, it reads as clearly as the day it was cut.

![Deeply carved hieroglyphs along a colonnade at Medinet Habu](/images/articles/medinet-habu-meaning-carved-walls.jpg)
*Cut deep into the stone, so the shadows keep the words.*

Some things in a life are only painted on. They fade with the first hard season. Others are carved: the promises you keep, the values you return to, the work you do with your whole heart.

Those are the ones still readable years from now.

## Guard what is sacred

Then there is the gate.

Unusually for an Egyptian temple, you enter Medinet Habu through a gatehouse modelled on a fortified Syrian tower, a **migdol**. It was probably more ceremonial than military. Around the temple, a mudbrick wall [is thought to have stood some 18 metres high](https://homepages.bluffton.edu/~SULLIVANM/egypt/thebes/medhabu/medhabu.html).

Whether or not they were ever meant for war, those walls drew a clear line between the noise of the world and the quiet inside. And at the very centre, protected by all of it, stood the small temple on the first hill.

Anything precious needs that line. A garden needs a wall. A mind needs a quiet place. Whatever you hold sacred needs protecting from everything that would wear it away.

## Battles outside, peace within

Walk around the outside of Medinet Habu and the walls are at war. On the great gateway, Ramesses III strikes down his enemies before the god Amun. Along the north wall, his fleet meets the Sea Peoples in one of the earliest sea battles ever depicted: ships locked together, enemies thrown into the water.

Step inside, and the mood changes. Deeper in, the war is shown only as an offering, with captives presented to the gods. Then come festivals, processions and gifts. By the inner halls, the walls are about worship, not war.

This order was deliberate. Egyptian temples wore their battles on the outside, like a shield: the king holding chaos at the border so that order could live within. The fight belongs at the edges. The centre stays still.

> Fight at the edges, so the centre can stay still.

### What power is for

To the Egyptians, power was never for its own sake. A king's strength had one purpose: to protect maat, the order that let life go on inside the walls. A ruler who used it any other way had failed at his only real job.

It is still the right question to ask of any power, in a family, a business or a life: what is it protecting? And the temple's layout works inside a person too. There will always be battles at the edges: pressure, noise, other people's demands. Meet them there, firmly, and keep a quiet place within that they never reach.

### The kingdom within

Every pharaoh carried the title **Lord of the Two Lands**: Upper and Lower Egypt, held together as one. That was the outer kingdom, with its borders, its armies and its walls.

Egyptian teaching also spoke of a kingdom closer to home. To the Egyptians, the heart was the seat of thought, will and conscience, and it was the heart, not the crown, that was weighed after death.

Their wisdom books praised one kind of person above all: the "silent man", calm, modest and in command of himself. The [Instruction of Amenemope](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instruction_of_Amenemope), most likely written down in the same Ramesside age as this temple, sets him against the "heated man", ruled by temper and grievance. One governs his own heart. The other is governed by everything around him.

Medinet Habu is built the same way: strength on the outside, stillness at the centre. A king held the Two Lands. Each of us holds a smaller kingdom, and it is ruled from within.

---

## The women who served the hidden god

Just inside the gate, on your left, stand the chapels of the **God's Wives of Amun**.

For around two centuries, the highest religious office in Thebes belonged to a woman. She gave her life to Amun. She did not marry. She ran great temple estates, and she chose her successor herself, by adopting her.

These women asked to rest here, beside the sacred hill. Their chapels are a quiet reminder that this ground was held by women of real spiritual authority, not only by kings.

## Holy ground stays holy

A shrine already stood here by the Middle Kingdom, around 2000 BC. [Hatshepsut](/experiences/hatshepsut-temple), the queen who ruled as king, rebuilt it with Thutmose III, and others kept adding to it for more than a thousand years. When times turned dangerous, people moved inside the great walls for safety, and a town called **Djeme** grew there, named after the sacred hill. Later, Christians built a church in the temple courtyard.

Each generation came for its own reasons. All of them came to the same ground.

People do not return to a place for two thousand years unless it gives them something. Outside, the world was always busy, always changing. Here stood something built to last. To stand inside it for a while was to remember that some things do.

![Visitors walking towards the gateway of Medinet Habu in the morning](/images/articles/medinet-habu-meaning-approach.jpg)
*Still walking towards the same gate.*

---

## What we know, and what we feel

Everything here comes from what the Egyptians carved and wrote themselves, and from a century of patient study. Since 1924, the [University of Chicago's Epigraphic Survey](https://isac.uchicago.edu/chicagonile) has copied these walls line by line. Scholars still debate the details, as they should. Our full list of sources is at the end of this page.

What no one can tell you is exactly how it felt to stand here three thousand years ago.

You will read claims online about energy fields and lost technology at Medinet Habu. They are not in the ancient sources, and we won't put words in the mouths of people who left us so many of their own.

But you don't need a theory to feel what so many feel here. A place loved for this long holds a particular stillness. You notice it most in the early morning, before anyone else arrives.

## How to walk it

- **Come at opening, usually 6am.** Soft light, cool stone, silence. The quiet is part of the visit.
- **Turn right first, to the small temple of Amun.** Begin where the Egyptians began.
- **Then the chapels of the God's Wives,** on your left inside the gate.
- **Walk the great temple slowly,** from bright courts into darker halls. Feel the light change.
- **Look up.** The colour survives best in the ceilings and the shadows.
- **Give it two hours,** and leave time simply to sit.
- **Make it a West Bank morning.** The Valley of the Kings is about fifteen minutes away; here is [how we choose which of its tombs are worth it](/insiders-guide/valley-of-the-kings-which-tombs).

## What most people miss

The beginning.

They come for the size and the battles, and leave without ever standing on the ground that made this place holy. The small temple is plain, a little worn, easy to pass by.

That is exactly why it is worth stopping for.

If you would like to begin there, at the quiet hour, with someone who knows what you are standing on, [this is how we arrange a morning at Medinet Habu](/medinet-habu). And if it is one part of a longer journey through the temples, [we can shape the whole day around it](/concierge-day).
